1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:05,525
The Nanyang Volunteer Drivers
of the Burma Road, 1939

2
00:00:05,525 --> 00:00:09,537
On the 18th of February 1939,
the Nanyang Siang Pau told

3
00:00:09,617 --> 00:00:13,409
its readers that a group of
Singapore's motor drivers

4
00:00:13,489 --> 00:00:17,800
would leave that day "to return
to the homeland and serve."

5
00:00:17,800 --> 00:00:21,146
They were the first 80
of about 3,200 young men,

6
00:00:21,226 --> 00:00:25,357
and a few women, who left
Singapore, Malaya and the rest of

7
00:00:25,437 --> 00:00:29,426
South-East Asia that year to
drive and repair the lorries

8
00:00:29,506 --> 00:00:33,208
on the Burma Road, the
mountain route that had become

9
00:00:33,288 --> 00:00:37,000
China's last open supply
line in its war with Japan.

10
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:39,725
Around a third of them died.

11
00:00:39,725 --> 00:00:44,500
Their story is usually told
as one of loyalty to China.

12
00:00:44,500 --> 00:00:47,498
It is also the starting
point of a change in

13
00:00:47,578 --> 00:00:51,075
where the Chinese of
Singapore thought of as home.

14
00:00:51,075 --> 00:00:55,777
When fighting broke out between
China and Japan in July 1937,

15
00:00:55,857 --> 00:00:57,894
the Chinese of the Nanyang,

16
00:00:57,974 --> 00:01:02,600
the Chinese name for South-East
Asia, responded with money.

17
00:01:02,600 --> 00:01:06,450
In Singapore, community
leaders led by the rubber

18
00:01:06,530 --> 00:01:10,140
magnate Tan Kah Kee set
up the Singapore China

19
00:01:10,220 --> 00:01:14,150
Relief Fund Committee on
the 15th of August 1937.

20
00:01:14,150 --> 00:01:17,277
According to the National
Archives of Singapore,

21
00:01:17,357 --> 00:01:21,886
its leadership cut across the dialect
groups, and rich and poor gave,

22
00:01:21,966 --> 00:01:25,962
from thousands of dollars to a
few cents, while students sold

23
00:01:26,042 --> 00:01:30,050
paper flowers and drama troupes
put on shows to raise funds.

24
00:01:30,050 --> 00:01:34,445
In October 1938, delegates from
across the region met at the

25
00:01:34,525 --> 00:01:38,696
Chinese High School in Singapore
and formed a region-wide

26
00:01:38,776 --> 00:01:43,325
federation of relief funds, with
Tan Kah Kee as its chairman.

27
00:01:43,325 --> 00:01:47,225
By then Japan held most of China's coast.

28
00:01:47,225 --> 00:01:51,042
Shanghai and Amoy had
fallen, and the loss of

29
00:01:51,122 --> 00:01:55,625
Canton in October 1938 cut
the last major sea route.

30
00:01:55,625 --> 00:01:59,104
China's government turned
to an overland route:

31
00:01:59,184 --> 00:02:04,101
the Burma Road, built in about
nine months by labourers in Yunnan,

32
00:02:04,181 --> 00:02:07,736
which ran 1,146 kilometres
through the mountains

33
00:02:07,816 --> 00:02:11,450
from the railhead at
Lashio in Burma to Kunming.

34
00:02:11,450 --> 00:02:14,820
Supplies shipped to
Rangoon could travel north

35
00:02:14,900 --> 00:02:18,425
by rail to Lashio and
then by lorry into China.

36
00:02:18,425 --> 00:02:22,316
What China lacked were enough
trained drivers and mechanics

37
00:02:22,396 --> 00:02:26,300
to run the convoys, and it
asked Tan Kah Kee to find them.

38
00:02:26,300 --> 00:02:29,486
The appeal went out in February 1939,

39
00:02:29,566 --> 00:02:31,950
and the response was quick.

40
00:02:31,950 --> 00:02:37,167
The first group, 80 men, left
Singapore on the 18th of February,

41
00:02:37,247 --> 00:02:42,875
and by August about 3,200 volunteers
had gone in successive batches.

42
00:02:42,875 --> 00:02:47,052
The National Archives of
Singapore counts nine batches;

43
00:02:47,132 --> 00:02:50,150
Chinese archival records count fifteen.

44
00:02:50,150 --> 00:02:53,578
Almost nine in ten came
from British Malaya,

45
00:02:53,658 --> 00:02:56,050
which then included Singapore.

46
00:02:56,050 --> 00:02:59,643
Many were experienced drivers
and mechanics who gave

47
00:02:59,723 --> 00:03:03,325
up better-paid work and
the comforts of home to go.

48
00:03:03,325 --> 00:03:07,830
Accounts also record a small number
of women, among them Li Yuemei,

49
00:03:07,910 --> 00:03:11,798
who was praised as a modern
Hua Mulan, after the legendary

50
00:03:11,878 --> 00:03:17,272
woman who disguised herself as a man to
fight, and a few non-Chinese volunteers,

51
00:03:17,352 --> 00:03:20,500
including Sikh brothers
from Taiping in Perak.

52
00:03:20,500 --> 00:03:25,040
The Nanyang Siang Pau of the
18th of February 1939 reported

53
00:03:25,120 --> 00:03:29,034
the first group's departure
on its local news page,

54
00:03:29,114 --> 00:03:33,500
beside a speech by Tan Kah
Kee to the departing drivers.

55
00:03:33,500 --> 00:03:36,495
Its headline suggested that their example

56
00:03:36,575 --> 00:03:39,950
might move the wealthy
to give twice as much.

57
00:03:39,950 --> 00:03:43,409
Two days later, the paper
described how the first

58
00:03:43,489 --> 00:03:47,164
group had organised itself,
with its own leaders and

59
00:03:47,244 --> 00:03:51,000
its own officers for finance,
records and publicity.

60
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:54,566
In China the volunteers had
about a month of training

61
00:03:54,646 --> 00:03:59,381
in assembling and repairing vehicles,
along with basic military drill,

62
00:03:59,461 --> 00:04:01,800
and then went to work on the road.

63
00:04:01,800 --> 00:04:05,356
It wound over mountain
passes and across the gorges

64
00:04:05,436 --> 00:04:09,134
of the Salween and Mekong
rivers, much of it unpaved,

65
00:04:09,214 --> 00:04:12,850
and it was prone to landslides
in the rainy season.

66
00:04:12,850 --> 00:04:15,664
Malaria was common in the river valleys,

67
00:04:15,744 --> 00:04:19,650
and Japanese aircraft bombed
the road and its bridges.

68
00:04:19,650 --> 00:04:24,916
By 1940 the road was carrying an
average of about 20,000 tons of

69
00:04:24,996 --> 00:04:30,425
goods a month into China, according
to the historian Rana Mitter.

70
00:04:30,425 --> 00:04:34,400
Sources differ on how
many volunteers died.

71
00:04:34,400 --> 00:04:38,520
One volunteer recalled that a
third of them died in service,

72
00:04:38,600 --> 00:04:40,900
the figure most histories now use,

73
00:04:40,980 --> 00:04:44,200
and some accounts put
the toll at nearly half.

74
00:04:44,200 --> 00:04:48,325
Accidents, disease and air
raids all took their share.

75
00:04:48,325 --> 00:04:53,750
When Japan occupied Burma in
May 1942, the road was cut.

76
00:04:53,750 --> 00:04:58,533
Some volunteers went on to work on
the airlift from India into China,

77
00:04:58,613 --> 00:05:02,700
and others joined the Chinese
army or Allied intelligence.

78
00:05:02,700 --> 00:05:07,295
By the end of the war, about a
third of the volunteers had died,

79
00:05:07,375 --> 00:05:10,801
about a third later
returned to South-East Asia,

80
00:05:10,881 --> 00:05:15,775
the first of them in 1946, and the
remaining third stayed in China.

81
00:05:15,775 --> 00:05:19,182
The war the volunteers had gone to fight

82
00:05:19,262 --> 00:05:22,400
reached their home in December 1941.

83
00:05:22,400 --> 00:05:25,743
At the colonial government's
request, Tan Kah Kee

84
00:05:25,823 --> 00:05:29,236
formed the Singapore Chinese
Mobilisation Council,

85
00:05:29,316 --> 00:05:33,148
and from it came Dalforce, a
volunteer force named after

86
00:05:33,228 --> 00:05:37,000
its British commander,
Lieutenant Colonel John Dalley.

87
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:39,920
According to the National Library Board,

88
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:44,795
between 2,000 and 4,000 people
volunteered, among them labourers,

89
00:05:44,875 --> 00:05:48,470
students and rickshaw
pullers, although there was

90
00:05:48,550 --> 00:05:52,450
time to form only eight
companies of about 150 each.

91
00:05:52,450 --> 00:05:57,112
Dalforce units went into position
from the 5th of February 1942

92
00:05:57,192 --> 00:06:01,478
at places such as Jurong Road,
Lim Chu Kang and Woodlands,

93
00:06:01,558 --> 00:06:06,300
and fought alongside Allied troops
in the battle for Singapore.

94
00:06:06,300 --> 00:06:10,120
After the surrender on
the 15th of February 1942,

95
00:06:10,200 --> 00:06:16,169
the Japanese military carried out the
Sook Ching, a screening of Chinese men

96
00:06:16,249 --> 00:06:21,025
aged 18 to 50 from the 21st of
February to the 4th of March.

97
00:06:21,025 --> 00:06:25,399
Among its targets were people on
lists of anti-Japanese suspects,

98
00:06:25,479 --> 00:06:28,415
and the community's
support for China's war,

99
00:06:28,495 --> 00:06:32,675
including the relief fund, was
one reason it was singled out.

100
00:06:32,675 --> 00:06:35,375
Many thousands were killed.

101
00:06:35,375 --> 00:06:38,800
Estimates of the number vary widely.

102
00:06:38,800 --> 00:06:42,260
Tan Kah Kee himself had
left before the fall

103
00:06:42,340 --> 00:06:45,800
and spent the Occupation
in hiding in Java.

104
00:06:45,800 --> 00:06:50,550
After the war, the question of
where home lay became practical.

105
00:06:50,550 --> 00:06:54,231
Tan Kah Kee went back to China in 1949,

106
00:06:54,311 --> 00:07:00,675
returned to Singapore in February
1950 and left again in May 1950.

107
00:07:00,675 --> 00:07:05,400
The colonial authorities then
refused to let him back in.

108
00:07:05,400 --> 00:07:09,275
He died in Beijing in 1961.

109
00:07:09,275 --> 00:07:12,008
Other families, including many who had

110
00:07:12,088 --> 00:07:15,050
given to the relief fund, chose to stay.

111
00:07:15,050 --> 00:07:18,823
In 1957 the Singapore
Citizenship Ordinance

112
00:07:18,903 --> 00:07:23,213
opened citizenship to most
of the roughly 220,000

113
00:07:23,293 --> 00:07:27,325
Chinese residents who
had been born in China.

114
00:07:27,325 --> 00:07:31,054
When the registration drive closed on the

115
00:07:31,134 --> 00:07:35,328
31st of January 1958,
more than 320,000 people

116
00:07:35,408 --> 00:07:39,125
had registered as citizens of Singapore.

117
00:07:39,125 --> 00:07:43,218
Among the community leaders who
took it up was Lien Ying Chow,

118
00:07:43,298 --> 00:07:47,324
who had himself spent the war
in Chongqing and was one of the

119
00:07:47,404 --> 00:07:51,375
first to become a citizen, as
told in the post on his life.

120
00:07:51,375 --> 00:07:56,079
For decades after the war the
volunteers were largely forgotten,

121
00:07:56,159 --> 00:07:59,075
in China as well as in South-East Asia.

122
00:07:59,075 --> 00:08:03,253
Researchers began to
rediscover them in the 1980s,

123
00:08:03,333 --> 00:08:09,125
and their story returned to wide
public view in the 2000s and 2010s.

124
00:08:09,125 --> 00:08:13,619
In 2009 the National Archives of
Singapore staged an exhibition

125
00:08:13,699 --> 00:08:18,193
on them with archives in China,
drawing on its own oral history

126
00:08:18,273 --> 00:08:22,766
interviews with surviving
volunteers, and material from it went

127
00:08:22,846 --> 00:08:27,275
into its Second World War gallery
at the Former Ford Factory.

128
00:08:27,275 --> 00:08:31,371
In 2013, Singapore Chinese
community organisations funded

129
00:08:31,451 --> 00:08:35,694
a sculpture of the volunteers
in the grounds of the Sun Yat

130
00:08:35,774 --> 00:08:39,950
Sen Nanyang Memorial Hall,
with the government's support.

131
00:08:39,950 --> 00:08:43,657
Where it fits in the bigger
story: The Nanyang volunteers

132
00:08:43,737 --> 00:08:47,125
are usually remembered for
what they did for China.

133
00:08:47,125 --> 00:08:51,633
Their generation is also the one
whose sense of belonging changed,

134
00:08:51,713 --> 00:08:54,484
as the war that began in China arrived in

135
00:08:54,564 --> 00:08:58,446
Malaya and families who had
once thought of themselves as

136
00:08:58,526 --> 00:09:02,350
overseas Chinese chose to
make Singapore their country.

137
00:09:02,350 --> 00:09:05,888
The volunteers who answered Tan Kah Kee's

138
00:09:05,968 --> 00:09:09,675
appeal in 1939 belong to that history too.
